ably about answer both  when you're having your interview so  when you're working with ansible when  should you be using the curly brackets  and so just as a frame of reference and  there's often two different ways that  these kind of brackets are referred to  now they're either referred to as French  brackets or curly brackets either way  and what you'll be wanting to ask is  when would you use these specific types  of brackets within an sport and really  the answer comes down into two things  one is that it makes it easier to  distinguish strings and undefined  variables and the second is for putting  together conditional statements when you  are actually using variables in the  example we have here is this prints the  value of and we have food and we have to  then put in the variable conditional  statement of foo is defined as something  so what is the best way to make content  reusable and redistributable with  ansible and there's really essentially  three the first is to include sub-module  or another file in your playbook the  second is to import an improvement of an  include which ensures that a final is  added only once and then the third is  roles to manage the tasks within the  playbook so a question you will be asked  is provide a differences between ansible  and puppets so if we look at as well  it's a very easy agentless installation  it's based on Python you can configure  it with llamó and there are no support  for windows in contrast puppet is an  agent-based installation it's written in  Ruby the configuration files are written  in DSL and it has support on all popular  operating systems so we dig deeper into  the actual architecture and so it has a  much more simple architecture and it's  definitely a push  only architecture and contrast to puppet  it's a more complicated but more  sophisticated architecture where you're  able to have a complete environment  managed by the puppet architecture with  that we've reached the end of this video  on the i